        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Run the Stick Drift Detector again. The stick should hold 0.0%–0.5% at rest. If drift persists, repeat the cleaning or swap the other module.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: 'ps5-dualsense',
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: No (soldering-free module kits exist; controller is not designed for hot-swap)',
      warrantyNote: 'Opening the controller voids your warranty. If your DualSense is under the 1-year manufacturer warranty, request an RMA instead.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'xbox-wireless',
      name: 'Xbox Wireless Controller (Series X/S)',
      platform: 'Xbox Series X/S / Xbox One / PC',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer',
      intro: 'Xbox Series controllers drift when the potentiometer wiper track accumulates carbon dust or the spring tension wears. Cleaning and tension checks resolve most cases.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector with the controller wired via USB. Note which stick and axis shows offset at rest.' },
        { title: 'Clean the potentiometer housings', body: 'Open the shell, remove the stick modules, and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol through the sensor housings while rotating the stick fully.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'Until the module is clean or replaced, set a small in-game deadzone that covers the measured drift — see our game settings guides.' },
        { title: 'Replace the stick modules if needed', body: 'Series X/S sticks are soldered modules; replacement requires soldering or a module-swap service. If the controller is under warranty, prefer an RMA.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Run the Stick Drift Detector again. A healthy stick reads 0.0%–0.5% at rest on both axes.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: 'xbox-series-x',
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: No (soldered; the Elite 2 has tension adjustment instead)',
      warrantyNote: 'Opening the controller voids your warranty. Microsoft covers drift under the 1-year warranty — request an RMA first.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'switch-pro',
      name: 'Nintendo Switch Pro Controller',
      platform: 'Nintendo Switch / PC',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer',
      intro: 'The Switch Pro Controller is one of the most drift-resistant first-party controllers, but its sticks still use potentiometers and can drift after heavy use or debris ingress.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector in Bluetooth mode, then again wired, and compare the readings.' },
        { title: 'Clean the stick sensors', body: 'Open the shell and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol into the sensor housings while rotating the sticks. Switch Pro sticks are fully sealed modules — cleaning usually means replacing them.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'Set a small deadzone in-game to cover the measured drift while you source a replacement module.' },
        { title: 'Replace the stick module', body: 'Pro Controller stick modules are drop-in replacements (approx. $10–$15) with a ribbon cable — no soldering required.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector. The module should read clean at rest; also confirm the D-Pad registers diagonals without misinputs.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: 'switch-pro',
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: Yes (drop-in, ribbon cable, no soldering)',
      warrantyNote: 'Opening the controller voids your warranty. Nintendo offers drift repairs for Joy-Con; Pro Controller is covered by standard regional warranty.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'joy-con',
      name: 'Nintendo Switch Joy-Con (L / R)',
      platform: 'Nintendo Switch',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer (ALPS)',
      intro: 'Joy-Con drift is the most documented controller failure of the generation. The tiny ALPS modules wear fast; replacement modules are cheap and drop-in.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector with each Joy-Con connected. Note which Joy-Con (L/R) drifts — drift can be one-sided.' },
        { title: 'Clean the ALPS module', body: 'Pop the module open and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol into the sensor while rotating the stick. This temporarily revives many drifting Joy-Con sticks.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'As a stopgap, raise the in-game deadzone on Switch titles that expose it (e.g., Fortnite).' },
        { title: 'Replace the module', body: 'Joy-Con stick modules are drop-in replacements (approx. $8–$15 each) with a ribbon cable; no soldering. If under warranty, use Nintendo\'s free Joy-Con drift repair program instead.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector. The replaced stick should read 0.0% at rest. Also test the ZL/ZR and SL/SR buttons while you are at it.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: null,
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: Yes (drop-in, ribbon cable, no soldering)',
      warrantyNote: 'Nintendo offers free Joy-Con drift repairs in most regions — request a repair before opening your Joy-Con.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'ps4-dualshock-4',
      name: 'PS4 DualShock 4',
      platform: 'PlayStation 4 / PC',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer (ALPS)',
      intro: 'DualShock 4 drift follows the same pattern as DualSense: carbon dust in the ALPS potentiometer wipers. Cleaning and module swaps both work.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector wired via USB to rule out Bluetooth interference affecting the readings.' },
        { title: 'Clean the potentiometer housings', body: 'Open the shell, flush 99% isopropyl alcohol into the green sensor housings, and rotate the sticks fully while flushing.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'Set an in-game deadzone covering the measured drift as a stopgap while the controller dries or you order modules.' },
