        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector and confirm 0.0%–0.5% at rest on both axes.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: null,
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: Yes (drop-in, ribbon cable, no soldering)',
      warrantyNote: 'Opening the controller voids your warranty. Sony service varies by region; check your local PlayStation repair portal.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'dualsense-edge',
      name: 'PS5 DualSense Edge',
      platform: 'PlayStation 5 / PC',
      sensor: 'Replaceable potentiometer stick modules',
      intro: 'The Edge is the easiest controller to repair: Sony designed it with user-swappable stick modules. Drift = swap the module, no tools, no soldering.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector to confirm which stick module is drifting and how far off-center it sits.' },
        { title: 'Swap the stick module', body: 'Release the module latch on the underside, slide out the drifting module, and press in a new one (official modules approx. $19.99, third-party less). No tools needed.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'While you wait for a replacement module, set an in-game deadzone covering the drift.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector. A fresh module reads 0.0% at rest. Confirm both sticks and the Fn lever still register.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: 'dualsense-edge',
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: Yes — designed for it (latch release, no tools)',
      warrantyNote: 'Module swaps do not void the Edge warranty. Keep your modules; used modules sell for parts.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'xbox-elite-2',
      name: 'Xbox Elite Series 2',
      platform: 'Xbox Series X/S / Xbox One / PC',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer (adjustable tension)',
      intro: 'Elite 2 sticks are still potentiometers; tension adjustment masks early drift but wear is the same as standard Xbox sticks. Module replacement needs soldering or a service kit.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector wired, with tension set to your normal preference.' },
        { title: 'Clean the stick sensors', body: 'Open the shell and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol through the sensor housings while rotating the sticks.' },
        { title: 'Adjust tension instead of deadzone where possible', body: 'The Elite 2 lets you raise stick tension, which can mask low-level drift. Use in-game deadzone for anything left over.' },
        { title: 'Replace the stick modules', body: 'Elite 2 sticks are soldered; replacement requires soldering or a repair service. Under warranty? Request a Microsoft RMA.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector and confirm the stick holds center at your chosen tension.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: null,
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: No (soldered; tension adjustment only)',
      warrantyNote: 'Opening the controller voids your warranty. Microsoft covers Elite 2 drift under warranty in most regions.',
    },
    {
      slug: '8bitdo-ultimate',
      name: '8BitDo Ultimate (Hall Effect)',
      platform: 'PC / Switch / mobile',
      sensor: 'Hall effect (contactless magnetic)',
      intro: 'Hall-effect sticks do not wear like potentiometers — drift here is usually a factory centering offset or magnetic center bounce, both fixable in software without opening anything.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector over both sticks. Hall-effect sticks read near-zero drift; anything above 1% is unusual and points to calibration, not wear.' },
        { title: 'Re-centre via firmware', body: 'Use the 8BitDo Ultimate Software to re-calibrate the stick center point. No disassembly required.' },
        { title: 'Tune the deadzone in software', body: 'Set a small deadzone in the 8BitDo software or in-game to absorb magnetic center bounce.' },
        { title: 'Do not disassemble for cleaning', body: 'There are no potentiometer wipers to clean. If drift persists after recalibration and the controller is under warranty, request a replacement.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector to confirm the sticks hold center.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: 'hall-effect-generic',
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: No (Hall effect — no wear parts to swap)',
      warrantyNote: '8BitDo offers a 12-month warranty — use it before disassembling.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'scuf-instinct-pro',
      name: 'SCUF Instinct Pro',
      platform: 'Xbox Series X/S / PC',
      sensor: 'Potentiometer',
      intro: 'The Instinct Pro uses the same potentiometer stick tech as standard Xbox controllers inside a pro shell. Cleaning works; stick replacement needs soldering or SCUF repair service.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector to confirm drift is the stick and not the paddles or triggers.' },
        { title: 'Clean the stick sensors', body: 'Open the shell and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol through the sensor housings while rotating the sticks.' },
        { title: 'Adjust in-game deadzones', body: 'Set a deadzone covering the measured drift while you arrange a repair.' },
        { title: 'Arrange a module service', body: 'SCUF offers official repair services for stick drift. Third-party soldered replacements also work if you are experienced.' },
        { title: 'Re-test the fix', body: 'Re-run the Stick Drift Detector — confirm center, then verify the paddles and instant triggers still register.' },
      ],
      reliabilitySlug: null,
      moduleReplacement: 'Swappable modules: No (soldered; official SCUF repair service available)',
      warrantyNote: 'SCUF offers a 6-month limited warranty; contact support before disassembling.',
    },
    {
      slug: 'steam-deck',
      name: 'Steam Deck (LCD / OLED)',
      platform: 'SteamOS / PC',
      sensor: 'LCD: potentiometer · OLED: Hall effect',
      intro: 'Steam Deck LCD models use potentiometer sticks prone to the same carbon-dust drift; OLED models use Hall-effect sticks that do not wear. Identify your model before repairing.',
      steps: [
        { title: 'Measure the exact drift', body: 'Run our Stick Drift Detector in Desktop Mode or in the browser. Note your model: LCD (potentiometer) or OLED (Hall effect).' },
        { title: 'LCD only: clean the sensors', body: 'Open the backplate and flush 99% isopropyl alcohol through the stick sensor housings while rotating.' },
        { title: 'Update the controller firmware', body: 'SteamOS updates include stick calibration fixes — check for updates before hardware work.' },
        { title: 'Replace the stick module (LCD)', body: 'Steam Deck stick modules are drop-in replacements (approx. $15–$25, ribbon cable). OLED units rarely need any of this.' },
